+# Bun migration
+
+Use Bun 1.4.4. Install dependencies with `bun install --frozen-lockfile`.
+
+- `bun run run [name] [count]` replaces the previous run task.
+- `bash scripts/run.sh [name] [count]` remains the shell entry point.
+- `bun run check`, `make check`, and `bun run typecheck` run TypeScript with strict checking and no output emission.
+- `bun src/main.ts [name] [count]` runs the CLI directly. The shebang also uses Bun.
+
+The JSON schema, default arguments, name trimming, number conversion, environment reads, and invalid-count exit status (2) are unchanged. The `@lib/` alias moved from the import map to TypeScript paths, which Bun resolves at runtime.
+
+There were no runtime dependencies, tests, serve tasks, or build tasks in the original project. TypeScript 6.0.3 matches the supplied baseline checker; Bun type definitions are pinned to 1.4.4. `bun.lock` fixes their transitive dependencies. CI installs the pinned Bun runtime and uses the frozen lockfile before checking.
+
+## Environment and security
+
+Automatic `.env` loading is disabled in `bunfig.toml` and explicitly in the scripted runtime invocations, preserving inherited-environment behavior. Bun documents this configuration at https://bun.com/docs/runtime/environment-variables.
+
+The original runtime permitted environment access with `--allow-env` but denied filesystem, network, and subprocess access by default. Bun does not enforce that permission boundary: the program and any future dependency run with the operating-system permissions of the invoking account. Use an external sandbox when that isolation is required. The CLI itself adds no filesystem, network, or subprocess operations.
+
+Formatter and linter replacement are outside this migration's scope.
